        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Compressor vs Desiccant vs Peltier in an RV
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are three kinds of dehumidifier that make sense in an RV, and they suit different seasons. A compressor dehumidifier works like a small air conditioner: a fan pulls air over a cold coil, water condenses and drips into a tank, and the air is reheated slightly before it leaves. It is the most efficient way to remove a lot of water in warm weather, which is why every 20 to 50 pint home unit uses one. The weakness is cold. Many compressor units are rated only down to about 41°F, and their output falls well before that.
          </p>

          <p>
            A desiccant dehumidifier passes air through a slowly turning wheel coated in a moisture-absorbing material, then uses a heater to drive the water back out of the wheel into a tank. It removes less water per watt than a compressor in warm, humid air, but it keeps working when the camper is cold, and Ivation lists its units to 33°F. It also warms the air slightly, which helps in winter and hurts in summer. With no compressor inside, it is lighter and less fussy about being tipped in transit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A Peltier or thermoelectric mini uses a small solid-state cooling plate instead of a compressor. It is quiet, light and draws very little power, but it removes a small fraction of what a compressor unit does: Eva-Dry's largest model is rated at about 25 ounces a day in warm, very humid air, against 20 pints for the Cube 20. Minis are useful on battery power and in a bedroom area. They will not clear a fogged-up trailer, which is why this guide includes only one, the EDV-2400, plus a renewable silica gel box for closets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most owners end up with one main unit matched to how they camp. Summer campers on hookups want a compressor unit. Shoulder-season, winter and northern campers want a desiccant one. Boondockers living on solar and batteries accept a mini for overnight use and dehumidify properly when the generator or shore power is on. Our <a href="/best-small-dehumidifier/">small dehumidifier guide</a> compares the minis and compact compressor units in more detail.
          </p>

