        <div id="power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Amp Draw on 30 Amp and 50 Amp Shore Power
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 30 amp RV hookup gives you a single 120 volt supply, which works out to about 3,600 watts. That sounds like a lot until you add up what runs at once. A rooftop air conditioner commonly draws somewhere in the low to mid teens of amps while running, and more for a moment at startup. A microwave and an electric water heater element can each take 10 amps or more. Run all three and you have tripped the main breaker before the dehumidifier is even plugged in.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news is that a small compressor dehumidifier is a modest load. Sylvane lists the Midea Cube 20 at a maximum running current of 1.9 amps and the MAD22C1AWS at 2.56 amps, roughly 220 to 295 watts. That fits comfortably alongside the air conditioner and fridge on a 30 amp pedestal. The desiccant units are bigger: the Ivation 13 pint is listed at 470 watts on high, about 4 amps, and a spec database lists the 19 pint at around 670 watts, close to 6 amps. Use the low setting when the air conditioner is running.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 50 amp hookup is a different world. It supplies two 120 volt legs at 50 amps each, about 12,000 watts in total, so any dehumidifier here is a rounding error. The only thing to watch on a 50 amp rig is which leg the outlet you use is wired to, if your energy management system sheds loads by leg. Plugging the dehumidifier into an outlet on the leg that also carries the second air conditioner is the one way to make it the thing that gets shut off.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you camp on 15 or 20 amp household power through an adapter, the margins shrink further. A 20 amp circuit can support about 1,900 watts of continuous load. A compressor dehumidifier and the fridge are fine; add an air conditioner and you are at the limit. In that situation, run the dehumidifier while the air conditioner is off, or rely on the air conditioner itself to remove moisture, which it does as a by-product of cooling. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> covers running cost at home.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">POWER AT A GLANCE</p>
