        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Running a Dehumidifier on an Inverter or Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running a dehumidifier off-grid comes down to arithmetic. Divide the unit's wattage by your battery voltage to get the DC current, then add about 10% for inverter losses. A Cube 20 at roughly 215 watts pulls around 17 amps from a 12.8 volt lithium bank, or about 19 amps after inverter losses. A 100 amp-hour lithium battery would run it continuously for about five hours. A compressor unit cycles on and off with the humidistat, so real runtime is longer, but it is still a bank-draining load overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The desiccant units are worse on batteries. At 470 watts, the Ivation 13 pint pulls close to 40 amps DC on high, which would flatten a single 100 amp-hour battery in about two and a half hours. That is not a reason to avoid desiccant units; it means they belong on shore power or a generator. The thermoelectric EDV-2400 is the opposite case. At about 60 watts, it pulls roughly 5 amps DC through an inverter and could run for most of a day on the same battery, which is why it is our battery pick despite its tiny output.
          </p>

          <p>
            A compressor needs more power for a split second at startup than it does running, so size the inverter with headroom. Spec sheets for these units do not publish a surge figure, so a pure sine wave inverter of about 1,000 watts or more for a 20 to 22 pint unit is a sensible floor. Modified sine wave inverters can make compressors and their control boards run hot or hum, and some manufacturers do not support them. Check the inverter's continuous and surge ratings, not just the headline number.
          </p>

          <p>
            True 12 volt dehumidifiers exist, but the ones we found are small thermoelectric units with outputs in the ounces per day. There is no 12 volt compressor dehumidifier we would point a reader to. The practical off-grid plan is a mini on the battery overnight, a compressor or desiccant unit run hard while the generator or solar surplus is available, and good ventilation the rest of the time. Our <a href="/best-lifepo4-rv-battery/">LiFePO4 RV battery guide</a> and <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator guide</a> cover the power side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Size Dehumidifier an RV Actually Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dehumidifier coverage claims are written for houses, so ignore them in an RV. Midea rates the Cube 20 for 1,500 square feet, and a 30 foot travel trailer has well under 300 square feet of floor. On those numbers, any compressor unit is oversized. In practice, the moisture load in an RV is concentrated, comes in bursts from showers and cooking, and has to be removed from cold surfaces, so a 20 to 22 pint compressor unit is the right size for most travel trailers, fifth wheels and Class C motorhomes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step up to the Cube 35 when the RV is lived in full-time, holds a family, sits for weeks in a humid climate, or is a large Class A or fifth wheel with several slides. A bigger unit in a small space is not harmful; it reaches the target, switches off and runs less often. The real costs are weight, storage space and a bigger tank to carry out when it fills. A 50 pint home dehumidifier is too heavy and bulky for nearly every RV.
          </p>

          <p>
            For desiccant units, Ivation's coverage figures are more realistic for RVs: 270 square feet for the 13 pint and 410 for the 19 pint. Those ratings are measured at 68°F and 60% humidity rather than the DOE test, so they are not directly comparable with the compressor numbers, but they do match RV floor areas. A Class B van or a teardrop camper is small enough that the 13 pint or even a mini can cope, provided you ventilate while cooking.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple check tells you whether your unit is big enough. Put a cheap digital hygrometer in the living area and watch it for a few days. If the dehumidifier holds the RV at 45% to 50% relative humidity and cycles off, it is the right size. If it runs nonstop and the reading stays above 55%, or the windows still fog by morning, you need more capacity, more ventilation or both. Our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/">dehumidifier buying guide</a> explains capacity ratings in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Drainage While Parked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Emptying a bucket is a bigger chore in an RV than in a house, because you have to carry it through a narrow aisle and down the steps without slopping it on the floor. The Cube's stacked tank holds up to about 3 gallons on the 20 and about 4.25 on the 35, which means fewer trips but heavier ones: 3 gallons of water weighs about 25 pounds. A continuous drain hose is the better answer whenever you are parked for more than a night or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The easiest route is gravity into the shower pan or the kitchen sink, which drains to the gray tank. Camping World's advice is to set the unit on a counter above the sink so the hose runs downhill, and to keep the gray tank valve open on full hookups so the tank does not overfill. A unit sitting in the shower pan with a short hose into the drain works the same way. The Midea Cube's bucketless mode is designed for exactly this, with the unit on a surface above a drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            On dry camping or a site without sewer, remember that every pint the dehumidifier removes is a pint in your gray tank. A Cube 20 running near its rating could add more than 2 gallons a day, so on a long boondocking stay it eats into gray tank capacity alongside dishes and showers. In that situation, letting the tank fill and emptying it outside onto the ground is often more practical, where campground rules allow it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some owners drill a hole through the floor and run the hose straight outside. It works, but it is a permanent modification, it can let in pests and road spray, and in freezing weather the hose outlet will ice up and back water into the unit. Use the sink or shower first. If you want a pump to send water up and out a window, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">dehumidifier with pump guide</a> covers the larger units that have one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Winter Camping and Storage Condensation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winter is when RV condensation gets serious. The furnace keeps the inside warm, the walls and windows stay cold, and every breath and shower ends up as water on the glass. A compressor dehumidifier helps when the camper is held at a normal indoor temperature, because the air it pulls in is warm. It struggles in a cold bedroom at the back of the rig, and in a camper left at 45°F while you are out skiing, it will collect very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is where the desiccant units come in. Rated down to 33°F, the Ivation models keep pulling water from cold air, and the heat they give off is welcome. A good winter setup is the furnace set to a steady temperature, a desiccant dehumidifier draining to the shower, a roof vent cracked slightly, and a fan aimed at the worst windows. Insulated window covers or reflective panels reduce how cold the glass gets, which cuts the condensation at its source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage is a separate problem. An RV closed up for the winter with no airflow can grow mildew in a few weeks if it was put away damp, and a cover that blocks the vents makes it worse. If the storage lot has power, a desiccant unit on its humidistat, draining to a sink, will keep the interior dry, but only above freezing: a drain hose or gray tank that freezes can back water into the unit. Winterized plumbing also means the sink traps hold antifreeze, so check the drain still flows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Without power, use non-electric absorbers. Camping World recommends tubs of desiccant for unpowered storage, and a renewable unit like the E-333 works in wardrobes and bays. Put the RV away clean and dry, leave cabinet doors and the fridge propped open, and visit every few weeks to empty absorbers and air it out. Our article on <a href="/do-you-need-a-dehumidifier-in-winter/">whether you need a dehumidifier in winter</a> explains why cold weather is not automatically dry indoors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="travel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Securing a Dehumidifier for Travel
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier is one of the heavier loose objects in an RV, and an unsecured 30 to 40 pound box becomes a projectile under hard braking. Before you move, empty the tank completely, disconnect the drain hose and coil it, and stow the unit low and wedged so it cannot slide. The shower stall is the most popular spot, because it is enclosed on three sides and a spilled drip goes down the drain. A ratchet strap or bungee to a grab bar keeps it from walking around on rough roads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a compressor unit upright. The compressor holds oil that can migrate into the refrigerant lines if the unit is laid on its side, and most compressor appliance manuals tell you to stand it upright for a period before switching on if it has been tipped. Check your manual for the exact wait. Desiccant and thermoelectric units have no compressor oil, which is one reason they are easier to live with in a vehicle, though they should still be stowed so they cannot fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size matters more for storage than for drying. The Cube 20 nests to a 13.5 inch cube, which fits in many shower pans and some basement bays, and the Cube 35 is about 14.5 inches square. The upright MAD22C1AWS stands nearly 20 inches tall. The Ivation units are slim, at under 9 inches wide for the 19 pint. Measure the spot where it will ride before you buy, including the height under any shelf or door.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not mount a dehumidifier permanently unless you are prepared to plumb it. A unit screwed to a wall or shelf needs a fixed drain route, adequate airflow on its intake and outlet sides, and a dedicated outlet. Most owners are better served by a portable unit that rides in the shower and comes out at camp. For cooling and dehumidifying in one box in a small camper, our <a href="/best-air-conditioner-for-camper/">camper air conditioner guide</a> looks at units that do both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a compressor unit for winter camping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Compressor dehumidifiers chill air over a coil, and many are rated only to about 41°F. In a cold camper or a bedroom at the back of the rig, output collapses. If you camp in shoulder season or winter, a desiccant unit like the Ivation, rated to 33°F, keeps working where a compressor stops.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a mini to clear foggy windows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thermoelectric mini removes ounces a day, not pints. Two people sleeping in a trailer can put far more moisture than that into the air overnight. Minis are fine for a closet or for battery use, but wet windows in the morning call for a compressor or desiccant unit and better ventilation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Traveling with water in the tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Cube tank can hold about 3 gallons, around 25 pounds of water, and it will slosh out on the first corner. Empty the tank fully, disconnect and coil the hose, and stow the unit low in the shower or strapped to a grab bar before the wheels move.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a desiccant unit on a small inverter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Desiccant units use a heater, and the Ivation 13 pint is listed at 470 watts on high. On a 12 volt bank that is close to 40 amps DC, enough to drain a single 100 amp-hour battery in a few hours. Run desiccant units on shore power or a generator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Draining into a gray tank that cannot take it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On dry camping, every pint the dehumidifier removes ends up in the gray tank. A compressor unit running hard can add a couple of gallons a day. On full hookups keep the gray valve open; on a long boondocking stay, empty the tank by hand instead of hosing it to the sink.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

