        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a dehumidifier in my RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most RVs benefit from one. Breathing, cooking, showers and propane use put a lot of moisture into a small space, and cold walls and windows turn it into condensation. If you see wet windows in the morning, musty cabinets or mildew in corners, ventilation alone is not keeping up, and a dehumidifier is the next step.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for a camper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most travel trailers, fifth wheels and Class C motorhomes, a 20 to 22 pint compressor unit is enough, despite the much larger coverage claims on the box. Full-timers, families and large Class A coaches in humid climates are better served by a 35 pint unit. Small vans and teardrops can manage with a 13 pint desiccant unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a dehumidifier on 30 amp RV power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A 30 amp hookup supplies about 3,600 watts, and retailers list the Midea Cube 20 at a maximum of 1.9 amps and the MAD22C1AWS at 2.56 amps. That fits alongside the air conditioner and fridge. Larger desiccant units draw more, so run them on low while the air conditioner is on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run an RV dehumidifier on batteries?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only small ones for long. A 215 watt compressor unit pulls around 19 amps DC through an inverter, so a 100 amp-hour lithium battery lasts about five hours of continuous running. A 60 watt thermoelectric unit lasts most of a day. Desiccant units are best kept for shore power or a generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for an RV, desiccant or compressor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Compressor units remove more water per watt in warm, humid weather and suit summer campers on hookups. Desiccant units keep working in cold air, down to 33°F on the Ivation listings, and warm the air slightly, which makes them better for winter camping and powered storage. Many owners choose by the season they camp in most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I leave a dehumidifier running in a stored RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the storage has power and stays above freezing, and only with a drain route that cannot freeze or overflow. A desiccant unit on its humidistat draining to a sink works well. Without power, use non-electric absorbers and a renewable unit in closets, and check the RV every few weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put a dehumidifier in an RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In the main living area, near the kitchen and bathroom where moisture is produced, with clear space around the intake and outlet. A spot in or beside the shower pan lets it drain straight to the gray tank. Close doors to rooms you are not using so the unit is not trying to dry the whole rig at once.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

