          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most RV owners on hookups, the Midea Cube 20 is the dehumidifier to buy: a real 20 pint compressor unit that retailers list at about 1.9 amps, with a large tank, a gravity drain for the shower and a nested shape that rides in the shower pan between trips. The MAD22C1AWS does the same job for less if you have room for an upright box, and full-timers in humid climates should step up to the Cube 35.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you camp in the cold or need to keep a stored RV dry, buy the Ivation desiccant units instead, rated to 33°F, and run them on shore power. For battery-only boondocking, the Eva-Dry EDV-2400 is the honest choice, with small output to match its small draw. Whatever you buy, ventilate first, drain to the shower and empty the tank before you drive.
